 "**The series' register**\n- **Deadpan humour inside the setting**: Jason answers an unreadable item description with \"Question marks. That's enlightening. Do I have to pay a wizard to identify items?\" (Book 1, Ch1 L108).\n- **Modern references in a fantasy world**: he suggests the Builder and Knowledge \"play Mario Kart\" (Book 3, Ch57 L87) and likens a projected face to Emperor Palpatine (Book 9, Ch6 L17).\n- **Australian idiom**: \"G'day\" and \"mate\" (Book 1, Ch38 L55), \"Strewth\" (Book 2, Ch44 L130) and \"yonks\" (Book 13, Ch1007 L22).\n- **Bleeped profanity**: Nik's swearing is bleeped on the page (Book 11, Ch8 L102).\n- **Humour does not cancel stakes**: Jason jokes about kneeling to the Storm King and then answers a threat of execution flatly: \"Die on my feet\" (Book 7, Ch59 L81-83).\n\n**Forms of address**\n- Titles for officials: \"Instructor Trenslow\" and \"Instructor Spalding\" during an assessment (Book 1, Ch54 L36); \"Priestess Pellin\" (Book 7, Ch9 L18).\n- Shade uses \"Mr Asano\" (Book 8, Ch50 L82) and \"Master Nik\" (Book 11, Ch36 L43).\n- Messengers are addressed as lord whatever their sex (Book 9, Ch56 L13), and a Voice of the Will as \"Voice Kaal\" (Book 9, Ch56 L14).\n\n**Condensed voice notes**\n- **Jason**: sarcastic, deferential only when rank forces it, with a dry understatement when he is grieving (\"It's not a celebratory time\", Book 5, Ch20 L25).\n- **Rufus**: courteous and formal, apologising for discourtesy to a priestess (Book 7, Ch9 L18), gesturing with a sausage on a stick while explaining guild quotas (Book 7, Ch12 L22-23).\n- **Farrah**: lectures on any subject (Book 1, Ch23 L15-17) and is blunt when angry (\"Barbou belongs to me\", Book 6, Ch26 L9).\n- **Gary**: deep and growling (Book 1, Ch9 L121), plain about death (\"rattling around in a hole\", Book 11, Ch57 L60).\n- **Humphrey**: precise and a little shy about romance (Book 1, Ch47 L88-89), then crisp in command (Book 13, Ch958 L48).\n- **Sophie**: wary of kindness (Book 2, Ch1 L77-80), silent about pain (Book 2, Ch45 L69-72).\n- **Clive**: a lecturer who loses the thread and apologises (Book 11, Ch68 L748) and jokes about wrestling an eel (Book 1, Ch68 L92-94).\n- **Neil**: tentative before he commits (Book 2, Ch27 L46).\n- **Belinda**: delighted by her own power (Book 2, Ch32 L72) and teasing (Book 7, Ch6 L42).\n- **Danielle**: blunt and quick on a weakness (Book 1, Ch47 L89), certain that \"diplomacy is war\" (Book 12, Ch23 L79).\n- **Emir**: smooth and showy, fond of grand entrances (Book 3, Ch5 L9) and a polished aside (Book 3, Ch69 L509).\n- **Dawn**: formal, with a teasing edge (Book 5, Ch20 L21-24).\n- **Shade**: butler-formal courtesy (Book 8, Ch50 L82; Book 11, Ch36 L43).\n- **Zara**: courtly with a threat in the smile (Book 2, Ch69 L17-19).\n- **Boris**: measured and careful (Book 11, Ch68 L493-498).\n- **Emi**: sharp and political at thirteen (Book 5, Ch24 L3-5). **Nik**: foul-mouthed (Book 11, Ch8 L102). **Taika**: casual (\"bro\", Book 11, Ch68 L472).\n- **Mr North**: amused and unhurried (Book 5, Ch41 L33).\n- **Raythe**: terse and final (Book 9, Ch97 L80). **Knowledge**: \"detached and untouchable\" (Book 1, Ch46 L16).",
